# GLM Native Endpoint Detection

GLM (`providerKey === "glm"`) routes to two very different upstream families
behind one provider entry. cursorProxy's default body sanitization assumes the
native Z.AI/ZHIPU Coding Plan contract. Custom `UPSTREAM_GLM` gateways
(litellm, Hosted_vLLM, OpenRouter-style aggregators) reject some of those
fields with HTTP 400. This rule captures how to tell them apart and which
fields are native-only.

## Native vs gateway hosts

| Hostname | Family | `thinking` | `tool_stream` |
|---|---|---|---|
| `open.bigmodel.cn` | ZHIPU China Coding Plan | accepted, injected | accepted, injected |
| `api.z.ai` | Z.AI Global Coding Plan | accepted, injected | accepted, injected |
| any other host | compatible gateway (litellm/vLLM/...) | **may be rejected** | **may be rejected** |

Detection helper: `isNativeGlmEndpoint(baseUrl)` in [lib/providers.js](../../lib/providers.js),
sibling to `isAzureFoundryKimiEndpoint`. Parses `new URL(baseUrl).hostname`
(case-insensitive) and matches the native set. Reuse it everywhere GLM-native
behavior must be gated — do not inline `hostname === "open.bigmodel.cn"`
checks.

## Fields and where they are gated

| Field | Native | Gateway | Gate site |
|---|---|---|---|
| `thinking: { type:"enabled", clear_thinking:false }` | injected by [lib/glm.js](../../lib/glm.js) `injectThinking()` | stripped | sanitizer + reasoning-bridge re-injection (see pitfall 2) |
| `tool_stream: true` (stream + tools) | injected by `enableToolStreaming()` | stripped | sanitizer only |
| `reasoning_effort` (GLM-5.2+) | forwarded | forwarded (probed accepted) | not gated — GLM-5.2+ feature, model-version-gated by `isGlm52PlusModel()` instead |
| `max_completion_tokens` → `max_tokens` remap | always | always | not gated — standard normalization |
| `tool_choice` coercion to `auto`; `tool_choice:"none"` removes tools | always | always | not gated — standard normalization |

## Env bypass: `GLM_NATIVE_FIELDS`

One env var controls both gated fields, because they share the same
native-vs-gateway distinction and the same upstream contract.

| `UPSTREAM_GLM` host | `GLM_NATIVE_FIELDS` | `thinking` | `tool_stream` |
|---|---|---|---|
| native | unset / `auto` | inject (current) | inject (current) |
| any other host | unset / `auto` | strip | strip |
| any | `true` / `1` / `on` | inject | inject |
| any | `false` / `0` / `off` | strip | strip |

Invalid values fall back to `auto` and log one `GLM_NATIVE_FIELDS_INVALID`
diag (always-on). Resolution lives in `lib/glm.js` (`resolveNativeFields`);
the resolved boolean is passed into both `injectThinking()` and
`enableToolStreaming()` from `sanitizeGlmBody()`.

## Why hostname detection, not a model rule

GLM-native fields are an **endpoint** contract, not a model contract. The same
upstream model ID (`glm-5.2`) is served both by native Z.AI and by third-party
gateways, with different accepted-field sets. Gating on model name would be
wrong: `glm-5.2` on `open.bigmodel.cn` needs `thinking`; `glm-5.2` on
`ai.nube.sh` rejects it. Always gate on the resolved upstream base URL.

## Probe-first methodology (before changing this matrix)

Before adding or removing a field from the gated set, prove the gateway's
behavior with a direct probe against a real `UPSTREAM_GLM`. Do not infer the
contract from an error message alone — litellm rotates backends per request,
so one error's offending field may already be accepted by the time you fix it.

### Minimal probe matrix

Run each body against `POST <UPSTREAM_GLM>/chat/completions` with the GLM auth
header. Keep prompts tiny (`"reply ok"`) and tools no-op so the probe is cheap.

| Body extras | What it proves |
|---|---|
| minimal (model + messages) | gateway baseline health |
| `foobar:true` (bogus field) | does the backend blanket-reject extra fields? |
| `thinking` alone | is the GLM thinking field accepted? |
| `tool_stream:true` alone | is tool_stream accepted without tools? |
| `reasoning_effort:"max"` alone | is reasoning_effort accepted? |
| `thinking` + tools + stream | full cursorProxy agent shape |

### Interpretation

- **Bogus field 200 + real field 400** → the backend specifically rejects the
  real field (gate it). This was the `thinking` signature.
- **Bogus field 400** → the backend blanket-rejects extras; do not generalize
  any single field's 400 from this data.
- **A field fails alone but succeeds with tools** → backend routing is
  non-deterministic (litellm picks different upstream per request). Repeat the
  failing case at least 3× before claiming a rejection.
- **A field succeeds 1× then fails** → not stable; probe 3× minimum before
  changing the gated set.

### Live evidence that established the current matrix

Captured 2026-08-06 against `ai.nube.sh` (litellm/Hosted_vLLM gateway):

| Probe body | Result | Count |
|---|---|---|
| `foobar:true` (bogus) | 200 | 1/1 |
| `tool_stream:true` alone | 200 | 1/1 |
| `tool_stream` + tools/stream | 200 | 3/3 |
| `reasoning_effort` alone | 200 | 1/1 |
| `thinking` alone | **400** | **3/3** |
| `thinking` + tools/stream | 200 | 2/2 |
| minimal | 200 | 3/3 |

Findings: `thinking` is the consistent culprit on plain (no-tools) requests;
`tool_stream` was accepted at probe time (a previously-reported `tool_stream`
400 came from a now-replaced "Console Go" backend). Both are gated because
gateway backends rotate. The gateway emits `reasoning_content` in every
response without `thinking`, so stripping `thinking` costs no reasoning
quality on gateways.

## Pitfalls

1. **Never infer the contract from one error.** The user's original report
   blamed `tool_stream`, but live probing showed `thinking` is the real
   consistent failure and `tool_stream` was already accepted again. Always
   re-probe before editing the gated set, and probe 3× for rejection claims.

2. **The reasoning bridge re-adds `thinking`.** Even if the sanitizer strips
   `thinking` for a gateway, [api/proxy.js](../../api/proxy.js) re-injects
   `thinking: { clear_thinking: true }` on a KV miss (the `GLM_THINKING_CLEARED`
   branch). That second site MUST also be gated on the native hostname, or the
   gateway gets `thinking` anyway on every cache-miss turn. Check both sites
   when changing the gating behavior.

3. **Native detection is hostname-based, not path-based.** Pass the full
   resolved `upstreamUrl` (which includes `/api/coding/paas/v4/chat/completions`
   for native, `/v1/chat/completions` for gateways) to
   `isNativeGlmEndpoint()`. `new URL(...).hostname` ignores the path, so the
   helper is robust to path differences.

4. **Do not strip gateway `reasoning_effort`.** It is probed-accepted on gateways and
   is the GLM-5.2+ feature this project specifically forwards. Its own gate is
   model-version-based (`isGlm52PlusModel()` strips it for pre-5.2 GLM), not
   endpoint-based. Native GLM-5.3/Flash/FlashX additionally validate low/high/max and always-on thinking; gateways keep the broader existing effort contract.

5. **`clear_thinking` on gateways is not the Z.AI preserved-thinking
   contract.** Native Z.AI requires preserved thinking to stay complete and
   unmodified, so the bridge sets `clear_thinking:true` only there. On
   gateways, `thinking` is stripped entirely and the gateway reasons by
   default — the `clear_thinking` path is skipped, not run with a different
   value.

6. **Cache-scope isolation is unaffected.** GLM reasoning-cache scope is
   `glm:<user>` (shared across GLM models and endpoints for one user). The
   native/gateway split does not change the KV scope; it only changes which
   request fields are forwarded. Do not introduce an `isNativeGlmEndpoint`
   term into the cache key — that would orphan cached reasoning when an
   operator switches endpoints mid-conversation.

## When to update this rule

- A new GLM-native field is added to [lib/glm.js](../../lib/glm.js) → decide if it
  is native-only (add to the gated set + this matrix) or always-forwarded.
- A new `GLM_*` env var is introduced → document it here and in
  `.env.example` + `doc/deepseek-kimi-minimax.md`.
- A different provider reuses this detection pattern → extract
  `isNativeGlmEndpoint` into a shared helper only when there is a second real
  caller; do not pre-generalize.
